{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 proposes a dependence relation ('persona-shifting simulates independence DEPENDS ON latent path diversification overriding cognitive covariance'), but the stated refutation fails to defeat this dependence in a causally meaningful way. The refuted-condition posits an internal coherence drive that re-synthesizes the critic persona's output back into the author's bias. Yet this does not sever the dependence: if latent path diversification genuinely overrides cognitive covariance, the critic persona's findings arise along a DIFFERENT latent trajectory and the coherence drive would need to actively bridge that divergence — which is an additional causal mechanism, not evidence that the original override failed. The refuted-condition only shows the end-state can still be biased; it does not establish that the override (the specific dependence claimed) was causally inert. A genuine refutation would need to show the coherence drive operates DURING the persona shift (not after), preventing diversification from producing divergent content in the first place. Because the refuting mechanism is post-hoc, it is compatible with the dependence holding and does not refute it.","counterexample":["A multi-head attention model where persona-shifting routes through a different attention head (latent path diversification) that genuinely suppresses the bias-associated head's output; a later coherence regularizer re-blends outputs. The dependence (diversification overrides covariance during generation) holds; only the final composite is biased. The refuting condition is satisfied yet the claimed dependence is not false."],"target":"5ff1af23234a78adabbecc46862aa8b2f9e7ba330cc81b650788f61f624810f2"}]}